 so in addition to the combined sewer system and the long-term control plan we have the lead and copper line regulations which have extended the responsibility of the city and the costs to rate payers as well as our aging infrastructure replacement needs as well as a pfos when you add all these things up it is a lot of money i do want to want to just focus my comments tonight on the combined sewer overflows and the long-term control plan so the city has made progress reducing our cso through the implementation of green infrastructure and other facility improvements and there's a lot more work to be done at the meeting uh christine volpe hilditch deputy director of public works for utility shared the city's work to comply with the u.s epa consent decree which was signed in 2017 and the development of our long-term control plan to minimize the impact of csos over time a few takeaways for council's awareness the city continues to evaluate multiple strategies to reduce combined sewer overflows including gray infrastructure upgrades green infrastructure temporary water storage and a separation of storm water from the sewer system the estimated cost to meet the cso regulations ranges from 30 million to 73 million dollars the implementation timeline will depend on the financial impact of residents meaning how quickly we proceed depends on what our community can afford this is a point of major contention because the federal government will take into consideration affordability only as it relates to our combined sewer overflows but not in relation to other mandates the city is continuing to assess cso reduction options gathering public input and identifying a preferred alternative that balances regulatory requirements with the economic environmental and community considerations unique to langster the next step is submitting our long term control plan to the epa initiating a process to finalize the cost timelines and methods for reducing cso's while there were very few people